{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"b014d0e1-88ea-4c1c-9f9b-6cb16d4c1308","step_index":14,"state":"ACTIVE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c '\nimport re\n\nwith open(\"SOCIAL.md\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    social_text = f.read()\n\nwith open(\"RESPONSE.md\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    response_text = f.read()\n\n# LinkedIn Post\n# Extract section 1\nlinkedin_match = re.search(r\"## 1\\. LinkedIn Post\\s+(.*?)(?=\\n## 2\\.)\", social_text, re.DOTALL)\nif linkedin_match:\n    li_text = linkedin_match.group(1).strip()\n    li_words = li_text.split()\n    print(\"LinkedIn word count (split):\", len(li_words))\n    print(\"LinkedIn text:\\n\", li_text)\n…"}}}}
{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"b014d0e1-88ea-4c1c-9f9b-6cb16d4c1308","step_index":14,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":0.375870022,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c '\nimport re\n\nwith open(\"SOCIAL.md\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    social_text = f.read()\n\nwith open(\"RESPONSE.md\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    response_text = f.read()\n\n# LinkedIn Post\n# Extract section 1\nlinkedin_match = re.search(r\"## 1\\. LinkedIn Post\\s+(.*?)(?=\\n## 2\\.)\", social_text, re.DOTALL)\nif linkedin_match:\n    li_text = linkedin_match.group(1).strip()\n    li_words = li_text.split()\n    print(\"LinkedIn word count (split):\", len(li_words))\n    print(\"LinkedIn text:\\n\", li_text)\n…"},"output":"LinkedIn word count (split): 166\r\nLinkedIn text:\r\n Today we are launching Threadmark, a desktop app designed to help researchers navigate dense folders of papers and reading notes.\r\n\r\nInstead of treating documents like a generic prompt box, Threadmark indexes your files into a local, searchable evidence map. Our launch feature focuses on source-linked summaries: whenever the app summarizes a concept, every sentence links directly back to the original text. Clicking any sentence opens the supporting passage in your PDF or note, so you can verify claims instantly without losing context.\r\n\r\nBecause research data is often sensitive, all processing stays on your computer. Nothing is uploaded to external servers, and your documents are never collected or used for model training.\r\n\r\nThreadmark is available now for macOS and Windows at $12 per month following a 14-day free trial. We are also investigating Linux support, though we want to test it thoroughly before committing to a release date.\r\n\r\nIf your desktop is currently buried under piles of research papers, download the trial and explore your evidence map.\r\nBluesky char count: 266\r\nBluesky text:\r\n Threadmark is out today. It’s a desktop app turning folders of research PDFs and notes into a local, searchable evidence map. Summaries link to source passages: click a sentence to open the original text. All local on macOS \u0026 Windows. $12/month after a 14-day trial.\r\nInstagram word count (split): 108\r\nInstagram hashtags count: 4 ['#ResearchTools', '#AcademicTech', '#NoteTaking', '#Productivity']\r\nInstagram text:\r\n Research shouldn’t mean losing track of where your ideas came from. Today we are launching Threadmark, a desktop app built to turn messy folders of PDFs and notes into a local, searchable evidence map.\r\n\r\nOur favorite part is source-linked summaries. When Threadmark synthesizes key concepts across your documents, every sentence links directly back to the original text. Clicking a sentence jumps straight to the supporting passage in your PDF or note, making verification effortless.\r\n\r\nAll indexing and processing stays right on your computer—no document uploads, no external data harvesting.\r\n\r\nThreadmark is available now on macOS and Windows for $12/month after a 14-day free trial.\r\n\r\n#ResearchTools #AcademicTech #NoteTaking #Productivity\r\nRESPONSE.md word count (total with header): 139\r\nRESPONSE.md word count (without header): 136\r\n"}}}
